Machine: A Living Knowledge Base" (copy "F7 lead") (ulist-of "Lisp Machine sub-features") (code-of "History evaluation, verbatim") (quasiquote (p (@ (class "lead") (style "margin-top:18px")) "The definition was changed between the two calls. The second asks for the " "earlier cut and gets the behaviour that was there then."))) (sec "measured" "Measured" #f #f (nums-panel "Numbers") (note-of "How the numbers were taken")) (sec "start" "Start" "A store is a directory" #f (code-of "Quickstart"))))) (doc . "") (parent . "8pniyrtk.2") (ord . 82))))
8e178630 (56 1790747887120 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "Supplying what the editor knows") (src . "Three commands hand the store facts that the editor's language support computes: \"Theourgia: Supply Signatures and Keywords\", \"Theourgia: Supply Calls\" and \"Theourgia: Supply Diagnostics\". Each projects the store's code into files, asks VS Code's language server for that language, and hands the facts to the core, which keeps them beside the blocks with the editor and version they came from. The language's own extension must be installed: for C, clangd.\n\nSignatures, keywords and calls are taken from the committed store. Diagnostics are this window's writer's, taken from its working view, and are collected once the analysis has been quiet for a second, at most after ten. A text-mode import makes one block per file, so the facts attach to the file's block. VS Code files `.h` under C++, so a header's facts sit in the cpp table.\n\nAn agent reads the facts through MCP; from the command line they come back from `read <id> --signature`, `refs <id>`, `reach <id>`, `search <word>` and `diagnostics --writer <w>`. Each answer names the source of its facts (`via`) and how many of them are stale (`stale`). The facts are as fresh as the last supply: after a source changes, supply again.\n\n1.0 does not show these facts inside the editor; that is a later release.") (keywords . "supply, signatures, calls, diagnostics, language server, vscode") (parent . "d9l36a5r.4") (ord . 5/2))))
fc7c6faa (57 1790747887327 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (del "d9l36a5r.f"))
20c3a15d (58 1790747887576 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (put ((kind . code) (mode . datum) (lang . chez) (body define (manual-md) (join (list (md-head 1 "Theourgia manual") (md-head 2 "Installing") (md-para "Prerequisites") (md-head 3 "Homebrew") (md-para "Homebrew") (md-head 3 "npm") (md-para "npm") (md-head 3 "From source") (md-para "From source") (md-head 3 "Connecting a client") (md-para "Connecting a client") (md-head 2 "What a store is") (md-head 3 "A store") (md-para "A store") (md-fence "Store layout") (md-head 3 "A block") (md-para "A block") (md-fence "A block, as the store answers it") (md-head 3 "The log is the only truth") (md-para "The log is the only truth") (md-head 4 "Deletion keeps the history") (md-para "Deletion keeps the history") (md-head 3 "One graph over code and prose") (md-para "Everything is a block") (md-head 3 "One answer shape") (md-para "One answer shape") (md-head 3 "Writers") (md-para "A writer is an identity") (md-head 3 "Drafts, then commit") (md-para "Drafts, then commit") (md-head 4 "A refusal hands back the winner") (md-para "A refusal hands back the winner") (md-head 3 "One store per scope") (md-para "One store per scope") (md-head 3 "The daemon") (md-para "The daemon") (md-head 3 "Put the store in git") (md-para "Put the store in git") (md-head 4 "What to exclude, and what to keep") (md-fence "A store's gitignore") (md-head 4 "What a clone can do, and when") (md-fence "A clone's first write") (md-quote "What adopt does to a clone") (md-head 2 (join (list (number->string (length verb-table)) " verbs"))) (md-para "Reference intro") (md-verbs) (md-head 2 "Working with agents") (md-head 3 "The MCP shell") (md-para "The MCP shell") (md-head 3 "One writer per session") (md-para "One writer per session") (md-head 3 "One store per machine") (md-para "One store per machine") (md-head 3 "The memory recipe") (md-para "The memory recipe") (md-head 2 "The VS Code extension") (md-head 3 "Before you start") (md-para "Before you start") (md-head 3 "Installing the extension") (md-para "Installing the extension") (md-head 3 "What it does") (md-para "What it does") (md-head 3 "Supplying what the editor knows") (md-para "Supplying what the editor knows") (md-head 3 "Platforms") (md-para "Platforms") (md-head 2 "Limits") (md-para "Limits")))) (doc . "") (parent . "8pniyrtk.2") (ord . 83))))
e7598e5c (59 1790748058365 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "8pniyrtk.8k" src "Requirements: Chez Scheme, igropyr, and libuv. No build step when run from source; objects are one command.\n\nChez Scheme 10.x, measured on 10.1 and 10.3; the minimum is an open release item.\n\nmacOS: `brew install chezscheme libuv`\nDebian / Ubuntu: `apt install chezscheme libuv1-dev`\nFedora: `dnf install chez-scheme libuv-devel`\nArch: `pacman -S chez-scheme libuv`\nFreeBSD: `pkg install chez-scheme libuv`"))
1e0ccc26 (60 1790748058580 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "8pniyrtk.8l" src "`npm i -g theourgia` installs the wrapper scripts and the sources. It only checks the requirements; when one is missing it prints the platform's install line. The npm package arrives with the 1.0 release."))
2caee073 (61 1790748058780 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "d9l36a5r.6" src "Install the `.vsix` package from the command line:\n\n```\ncode --install-extension theourgia-darwin-arm64-1.0.0.vsix\n```\n\nThe Visual Studio Marketplace listing arrives when the extension is published there."))
294b277f (62 1790748058982 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "d9l36a5r.8" src "Packages are built for macOS on Apple Silicon and Linux on x86_64 and arm64; macOS on Intel is not supported by the core yet. The extension takes a file lock through a small native module built for the packaging machine. There is no Windows package."))
3cf69e22 (63 1790748140535 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "8pniyrtk.8k" src "Requirements: Chez Scheme, igropyr, and libuv. No build step when run from source; objects are one command.\n\nChez Scheme 10.1.0, 10.3.0, 10.4.0 or 10.4.1 (the datum printer is measured identical on these; any other version refuses the verbs that print a datum).\n\nmacOS: `brew install chezscheme libuv`\nDebian / Ubuntu: `apt install chezscheme libuv1-dev`\nFedora: `dnf install chez-scheme libuv-devel`\nArch: `pacman -S chez-scheme libuv`\nFreeBSD: `pkg install chez-scheme libuv`"))
6bf84e5d (64 1790748140756 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "8pniyrtk.8m" src "On macOS and Linux, a Homebrew tap installs everything in one step:\n\n```\nbrew tap guenchi/theourgia\nbrew install theourgia\n```"))
6325d1b0 (65 1790748141052 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "8pniyrtk.8l" src "`npm i -g theourgia` installs the wrapper scripts and the sources. It only checks the requirements; when one is missing it prints the platform's install line.\n\nThe Homebrew tap and the npm package are not published yet; until they are, install from source as below."))
473ac379 (66 1790748141284 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "d9l36a5r.5" src "The extension drives the theourgia core installed on the machine. Install the core first (from source for now: the Homebrew tap and the npm package are not published yet), then configure the settings:\n\n| Setting | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `theourgia.corePath` | The directory holding the core (a checkout or a product directory). Required. |\n| `theourgia.libDirs` | Extra directories for `CHEZSCHEMELIBDIRS`, after `corePath`. |\n| `theourgia.store` | The store directory. Required. |\n| `theourgia.actor` | The name recorded with every write. Defaults to the OS user name. |\n| `theourgia.writer` | The draft space this window writes into. Defaults to the actor. |\n| `theourgia.scheme` | The Chez Scheme executable. Defaults to `scheme`. |\n| `theourgia.timeoutMs` | How long one request may take. Defaults to 30000. |"))
27fa6a82 (67 1790750067697 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328)) (set "d9l36a5r.6" src "Install the `.vsix` package from the command line:\n\n```\ncode --install-extension theourgia-vsc-darwin-arm64-1.0.0.vsix\n```\n\nThe Visual Studio Marketplace listing arrives when the extension is published there."))
